    'tip.from': '{km} from {site}', 'tip.here': 'This is the site you are exploring.', 'tip.now': 'Now: {sun} · {earth}.', 'tip.action': 'Click the name to explore; the + to compare.', 'tip.limit': 'A regional reference, not a certified landing site.',
    'about.shackleton': 'Rim of Shackleton crater (21 km across), about 10 km from the south pole. The crater floor is in permanent shadow.',
    'about.malapert': 'Malapert massif, a mountain about 5 km high facing the Earth: good line of sight for communication.',
    'about.degerlache': 'de Gerlache crater region (32 km), next to permanently shadowed areas.',
    'about.nobile': 'Nobile crater region (about 73 km), chosen by NASA in 2021 for the VIPER rover.',
    'about.haworth': 'Haworth crater region (51 km), deep, with a permanently shadowed floor.',
    'data.title': 'Where the numbers come from',
    'data.text': 'Relief from the 20 m LOLA south pole model (NASA PGDA), on a ~130 m mesh over a 100 km tile, plus 400 km of context for the far horizon. Sun and Earth positions from Astronomy Engine, checked against JPL Horizons.',
    'data.limit': 'For education. These points are regional references, not certified landing sites.',
    'footer.made': 'An independent explorer of the lunar south pole.',
    'footer.data': 'Data: NASA PGDA · LRO/LOLA · NASA SVS · LROC. Spacecraft: NASA 3D Resources. Stars: HYG v4.1 (CC BY-SA 4.0). Ephemerides: Astronomy Engine. 3D: Three.js.',
    'about.close': 'Close', 'about.title': 'Relief and sky share one geometry.',
    'about.lead': 'Horisite combines measured relief with astronomical ephemerides. The points are regional references, not certified landing ellipses.',
    'about.terrain.t': 'Relief',
    'about.terrain.d': 'The 20 m LOLA south pole DEM (NASA GSFC PGDA, Barker et al. 2023), read from its 80 m overview and resampled into 100 × 100 km tiles of 769 × 769 points (~130 m). Around it, 400 × 400 km from the 240 m DEM for the far horizon. Lunar curvature and true vertical scale. Checked against the 474 m SVS DEM used before: mean difference about 8 m.',
    'about.positions.t': 'Positions',
    'about.positions.d': 'Astronomy Engine 2.1.19: geometric J2000 vectors and the approximate IAU lunar orientation, with observer parallax. In one reference case the largest difference from JPL Horizons was 0.0024°.',
    'about.visibility.t': 'Visibility',
    'about.visibility.d': 'Horizon every 0.5° of direction, with relief sampled out to about 195 km (detailed to 50 km). The Sun and the centre of Earth are compared with that profile. Obstacles smaller than the mesh are not included.',
    'about.energy.t': 'Energy',
    'about.energy.d': 'Approximate incident irradiance: 1361 W/m² at 1 AU times the visible fraction of the solar disc, after the relief and, in eclipses, the part covered by Earth (Earth’s radius enlarged by 1/85 for its atmosphere, Danjon’s rule). No panel efficiency, losses, temperature or storage.',
    'about.render.t': '3D image',
    'about.render.d': 'Regolith shading (lunar-Lambert model) and sun shadows. Near the observer, 20 × 20 km of the same DEM at 40 m. Micro-relief below the mesh and ground brightness variations are synthetic and visual only. The reading light is optional and not physical. Permanent shadow: the PGDA map of permanently shadowed regions (Barker et al. 2023). Stars from the HYG v4.1 catalogue to magnitude 6, at J2000 positions. Earth oriented by sidereal time with its real phase; day, cloud and night-light textures from the Three.js examples.',
    'about.mission.t': '3D mission',
    'about.mission.d': 'The Apollo 11 profile (parking orbits of 185 km at Earth and ~110 km at the Moon, 13 laps before the landing and 5 after the liftoff), with the bodies at true scale; manoeuvres and timings are illustrative. Earth with a physical atmosphere (Hillaire 2020) over the Blue Marble Next Generation of September 2004 (NASA Earth Observatory), lights from Black Marble 2016 (NASA GSFC, VIIRS), clouds from Blue Marble 2002 and the MODIS MOD44W water mask (NASA GIBS). Moon: the LROC WAC colour mosaic (NASA/GSFC/Arizona State University, SVS CGI Moon Kit) and LOLA relief, with synthetic small craters. Sea from a wave spectrum (Tessendorf; Elfouhaily et al. 1997). Kennedy Space Center from the air: USDA NAIP aerial imagery (USGS The National Map, public domain). Rendezvous and docking in lunar orbit as on Apollo 11: the final approach with RCS braking, then the docked stack. Stated licences: city lights, airglow and moonlight on Earth boosted to show; earthshine on the Moon’s night side boosted 400× in space; flames and plasma dimmer than real so the spacecraft stays readable; in the trajectory diagrams the sky shows as in a long exposure (~20 stops).',
    'about.walk.t': 'Walking',
    'about.walk.d': 'In the Surface view you walk the 800 × 800 m around the point at 1.2 m/s (2.5 m/s running, in low hops), eyes at the observer height. Jumps follow the Moon’s gravity, 1.62 m/s²: ~42 cm and ~1.45 s in the air, like John Young’s jump on Apollo 16. The ground follows the LOLA relief with synthetic craters and rocks, and your shadow and those of the rocks and craters come from the real sunlight. Sun and Earth are checked against the relief from where you stand; the panel numbers stay those of the reference point. The lunar module is NASA’s official 3D model (NASA 3D Resources), landed ~30 m from the point on the flattest spot nearby; its crumpled Kapton blankets are generated in the shader, and the flag and the “UNITED STATES” placard give way to the NASA Space Apps Challenge logo. The helmet is an illustrative frame, and the sun visor imitates the gold film of the Apollo visors. Sunlight on the relief is computed point by point from the real horizon toward the Sun, with the penumbra of the solar disc (0.53°); the regolith has Hapke photometry and the opposition surge around the shadow of your head, and the camera adjusts its exposure like a real camera. “Enter the lander” takes you to the Commander’s window, in the lunar module cabin rebuilt from the Apollo Operations Handbook (panels 1 to 4, DSKY, circuit breakers, hand controllers).',
    'about.windows.t': 'Windows',
    'about.windows.d': 'The longest continuous interval common to the chosen sites in the next 30 days, sampled every hour. What happens between samples is not resolved.',
    'about.limits': 'For exploration and education. A mission decision needs finer illumination analysis, SPICE orientation and link validation. There is no operational certification.',
    'mission.title': 'Your window, one mission.', 'mission.close': 'Close simulation',
